<section id="about" class="part">
<h2>What this page is</h2>
<p>The <code>fortress-repo</code> skill holds the rules that bind every worker in the repository. This page shows you each change that landed in it, as a diff with its reason, so that you can check what the workers are now told whenever you want. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat.</p>
</section>

<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Not yet read</h2>
<p>Eleven items, all new. The page holds only what you have not read. At 06:38 UTC on 7 November, your comment &ldquo;LGTM&rdquo; on change 36 marked the page read up to it, so change 36 left, and the new items are numbered from 37.</p>
<p>Climb batch 27 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 16:18 and 16:28 UTC on 7 November. Changes 37 to 41 are the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the update rewrote three of its sections for the landing, its judge said to install all three, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 42 to 47 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk, the library and the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
<p>Five items are new rules: change 37, a program's trait with its own <code>+</code> excludes <code>Number</code>; change 39, a type without its own <code>=</code> compares by strict equivalence; change 40, the static argument written where walk's printed type misleads; change 41, <code>Number</code> declares nothing; change 42, walk's float numerals under strict equivalence. Change 38 narrows a rule: the <code>Index</code> bound goes only into code that walk runs or that the checker checks against the interpreter's library. The other five state facts that the batch changed, and change no rule.</p>
<p>Each item shows the text on <code>main</code> now, against the text before the first of the three commits. The words that came in are underlined, and the words that went are struck through.</p>

<section id="l37" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 37 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Dispatch and overloading&rdquo;</span>A program's trait with its own + excludes Number</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Dispatch and overloading&rdquo;, line 24, the paragraph on the Meet Rule</p>
<p>The Meet Rule asks for a declaration that covers the meet of two overloads that neither exclude each other nor nest (<code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Fortress as a language&rdquo;). An abstract functional method also counts for it. So the checker refuses <code>h(self): ZZ32</code> in a trait <code>S</code> beside a top-level <code>h(x: String): ZZ32</code>: nothing covers the meet of <code>S</code> and <code>String</code>. <span class="add">Likewise, a program's trait that declares its own functional <code>+</code> overlaps the library's <code>+</code> between an array and a number, because a type can extend both the trait and <code>Number</code>, which has no <code>comprises</code> clause (P-160). Declare the trait <code>excludes { Number }</code>, or make it exclude arrays. Otherwise walk refuses the program at load: &ldquo;fails the Meet Rule&rdquo;. The checker refuses such a pair in one component, but accepts it against the library's <code>+</code> (row 918). Give a trait with its own functional <code>-</code>, <code>MIN</code> or <code>MAX</code> the same exclusion: the library declares these in the same form.</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. Your decision opened the library's <code>Number</code>: the batch removed its <code>comprises</code> clause, so that a program may declare its own number type below it. Since then, one type can extend both <code>Number</code> and a program's own trait. So a trait that declares its own functional <code>+</code> overlaps the library's <code>+</code> between an array and a number, and walk refuses the whole program at load, with &ldquo;fails the Meet Rule&rdquo;. The checker accepts the same program (ledger row 918). The old text did not warn of this. The new sentences give the fix: the trait excludes <code>Number</code>, or arrays. They give <code>-</code>, <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> the same fix, since the library declares them in the same form. Only <code>+</code> was measured, but an extra exclusion cannot make a valid program invalid. The first wording gave the reason as &ldquo;<code>Number</code> is open&rdquo;, a term that the part did not define. After the cold read, it says that a type can extend both the trait and <code>Number</code>, which has no <code>comprises</code> clause.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l38" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 38 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>Where to write the Index bound</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, line 48, the paragraph on the <code>Index</code> bound: its first sentences, then its last sentence before its two departures; the sentences between did not change</p>
<p><strong>An indexed type's index parameter is bounded by <code>Index</code> (P-156).</strong> An indexed type is a type whose values <code>a[i]</code> subscripts, such as an array. Its index parameter is the static parameter that gives the type of <code>i</code>. In the library, and in your own generic over such a type, write <code>I extends Index</code>. Walk runs the code without the bound. The checker refuses it when it checks against the interpreter's library, as the count stage does (<code>compiler.md</code>). &hellip; Until the switch-over, the compiler's prelude declares no <code>Index</code>, so a program that names it does not compile<span class="cut">.</span><span class="add">: write the bound only in code that runs under walk or is checked against the interpreter's library.</span> Two departures:</p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A change of a rule's meaning: the rule now holds only where the tree can follow it. The paragraph told a worker to write <code>I extends Index</code> in its own generic over an indexed type. Its last sentence said that, until the switch-over, a program that names <code>Index</code> does not compile on the compiled path. So for a compiled program the two sentences gave opposite orders. The orientation update's own cold read found this. The last sentence now gives the action: write the bound only in code that runs under walk, or that the checker checks against the interpreter's library. The update wrote this as a sentence of its own, which began &ldquo;So write the bound now&rdquo;. After the skill's cold read, it became the end of the sentence before it, and the loose &ldquo;now&rdquo; went.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l39" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 39 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>A type without its own = compares by strict equivalence</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, lines 55 to 61, a new rule after the rule on pairs and triples</p>
<p><span class="add"><strong>Where a type declares no <code>=</code>, <code>=</code> is strict equivalence, <code>SEQV</code> (P-168).</strong> The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> answers <code>a SEQV b</code>. <code>SEQV</code> follows the specification on both paths:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span class="add">values of different types are never equivalent;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a value object is equivalent field by field, a tuple element by element, an integer, a character or a boolean by its value, and a float by its bits;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">an object not declared <code>value</code>, a string among them, is equivalent only to itself.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span class="add">So for a value object <code>V</code> without its own <code>=</code>, <code>V(s) = V(t)</code> is <code>false</code> where <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> are equal strings made by two computations. The library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so <code>(s, 1, 2, 3) = (t, 1, 2, 3)</code> is <code>false</code> too. Both <code>false</code> answers are the rule's: assert them as correct. Under walk a float numeral is a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, so it is not equivalent to a computed <code>RR64</code> (<code>interpreter.md</code>, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;). To compare by content, give the value object an <code>=</code>, or compare the parts. A string's <code>=</code> compares text. Under walk, <code>=/=</code> and <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> reach a type's own <code>=</code> only if the type extends a library trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code> (row 807). Otherwise write <code>assert(x = y, msg)</code>. Until the switch-over, the compiler's library declares no <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> and none on tuples. Its own operator <code>===</code>, the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>, compares strings and <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code> by <code>=</code>, not by bits or identity (row 908).</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. The batch made both paths answer <code>SEQV</code>, strict equivalence, by the specification's rule, as your decision on its meaning asks. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code>, which every type without its own <code>=</code> falls back to, answers <code>SEQV</code>. The old part said nothing of this. A worker would expect such an <code>=</code> to compare content, and would take the two <code>false</code> answers in the new text for defects. The new rule says how <code>SEQV</code> compares, gives those two cases and says to assert them as correct, and gives the fix. It also names walk's limit on <code>assert</code>, and what the compiler's library does until the switch-over. After the cold read, it says how an integer, a character or a boolean compares: by its value. It names a string's kind as &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo;, in place of &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;, which the skill does not define. It writes the two <code>false</code> cases as examples, in place of a string &ldquo;built apart&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply. It points to walk's float numerals (change 42). And it says what <code>===</code> is: the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l40" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 40 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Walk prints ZZ32 where the instance is Int</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, line 88, the paragraph &ldquo;Under walk a numeral has a run-time class&rdquo;, up to its sentence on walk's error messages; the sentences after it did not change</p>
<p><strong>Under walk a numeral has a run-time class.</strong> The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling under <code>Number</code> that converts to the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> (P-76). Walk gives a numeral the class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> by its value. These classes stand for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, but a generic instantiates at the class<span class="cut">: <code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code></span><span class="add">. Walk prints these classes as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside static arguments, so a printed type does not show the instance: <code>Cell(3)</code> prints <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> but is a <code>Cell[\Int\]</code>, and no <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code></span> (row 364). <span class="add">Where you need a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, write <code>Cell[\ZZ32\](3)</code>. Walk's own error messages say <code>Int</code>.</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule, beside a fact that changed. The batch changed how walk prints a type: it now prints its numeral classes <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> and <code>BigNum</code> as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside a static argument. The old example, &ldquo;<code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code>&rdquo;, named a type that walk no longer prints. A worker who saw <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> in walk's output would take the value for a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, and a dispatch on that type would then fail. The text now says that the printed name hides the instance. After the cold read, it says what to write where a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> is needed, the workaround that ledger row 364 records. It names the printed names outright, in place of &ldquo;the traits' names&rdquo;. And the sentence on walk's error messages lost a &ldquo;still&rdquo;, which told of a change rather than of what a worker sees.</p></div>
