<section id="l41" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 41 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Number declares nothing</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, line 92, a new rule after the rule on coercions</p>
<p><span class="add"><strong><code>Number</code> declares nothing (P-165).</strong> Each of the eight number types, and <code>IntLiteral</code>, declares its own <code>asFloat</code> into <code>RR64</code>. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call on a <code>T</code> no method or operator that the number types declare, <code>asFloat</code> among them. Two numbers of different types compare by <code>=</code> only where a coercion carries one into the other: the library declares <code>=</code> for each such pair, so a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value are <code>=</code>. Otherwise <code>=</code> is <code>SEQV</code> (&ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;), and answers <code>false</code> without an error: <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>false</code>, as is a <code>ZZ64</code> against an equal <code>RR64</code>. To compare values across types at known types, write <code>asFloat(x) = y</code>. In generic code over <code>T extends Number</code>, declare your function once for each number type instead. A program may also declare its own number type below <code>Number</code> (P-160).</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. By your decision, <code>asFloat</code>, and the <code>=</code> built on it, left the trait <code>Number</code>, and the batch made it so. Each of the eight number types, and <code>IntLiteral</code>, now declares its own <code>asFloat</code>, and <code>Number</code> declares nothing. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call nothing on a <code>T</code> that the number types declare. Two numbers of different types are <code>=</code> only where a coercion joins them. Otherwise <code>=</code> falls back to <code>SEQV</code> and answers <code>false</code>, with no error. The old part said none of this: a worker would have called <code>asFloat</code> on a <code>T</code>, or expected <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> to be <code>true</code>. After the cold read, the text gives a pair that a coercion joins, a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value, which are <code>=</code>. It says &ldquo;declare your function once for each number type&rdquo;, since &ldquo;the function&rdquo; could be read as <code>asFloat</code>. And its last sentence no longer says why a program may declare its own number type: change 37 now gives that reason.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l42" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 42 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;</span>Walk's float numeral is not equivalent to a computed RR64</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;, line 18, a new paragraph after the one on how walk chooses a coercion</p>
<p><span class="add">Walk keeps a float numeral as a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, also where it is bound to an <code>RR64</code> variable, parameter or field. So <code>SEQV</code> finds it unequal to a computed <code>RR64</code> of the same bits. This <code>false</code> follows the rule that values of different types are never equivalent, and no ledger row holds it (F-326). For <code>value object V(f: RR64)</code> with no <code>=</code> of its own, <code>x: RR64 = 0.25 + 0.25</code> and <code>y: RR64 = 0.5</code>, <code>V(0.5) = V(x)</code> is <code>false</code>. So is <code>(y, 1, 2, 3) = (x, 1, 2, 3)</code>, since the library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only. Give the value object its own <code>=</code> and make it extend <code>Equality[\V\]</code>, or compare the parts.</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule: a trap and its fix. Walk keeps a float numeral, such as <code>0.5</code>, as a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, also where it binds to an <code>RR64</code>. By the <code>SEQV</code> rule (change 39), values of different types are never equivalent. So a value object without its own <code>=</code> that holds such a numeral is not <code>=</code> to one that holds a computed <code>RR64</code> of the same bits, and a tuple of four or more is not either. The orientation update's judge ran this under walk and found it. The new <code>SEQV</code> rule alone would lead a worker to expect <code>true</code>, and the judge sent the point to this part. After the cold read, the text says that the <code>false</code> is the rule's answer and that no ledger row holds it, so that a worker knows not to record it as a defect. It says that the numeral stays a <code>FloatLiteral</code> at a variable, a parameter or a field. It writes the tuple case as an example, with its reason: the library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only. And the fix adds <code>Equality[\V\]</code>, since without it walk's two-value <code>assert</code> skips the type's own <code>=</code> and still answers <code>false</code>.</p></div>
</section>

