142	<section id="about" class="part">
143	<h2>What this page is</h2>
144	<p>The <code>fortress-repo</code> skill holds the rules that bind every worker in the repository. This page shows you each change that landed in it, as a diff with its reason, so that you can check what the workers are now told whenever you want. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat.</p>
145	</section>
146	
147	<section id="s1" class="part">
148	<h2>Not yet read</h2>
149	<p>Eleven items, all new. The page holds only what you have not read. At 06:38 UTC on 7 November, your comment &ldquo;LGTM&rdquo; on change 36 marked the page read up to it, so change 36 left, and the new items are numbered from 37.</p>
150	<p>Climb batch 27 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 16:18 and 16:28 UTC on 7 November. Changes 37 to 41 are the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the update rewrote three of its sections for the landing, its judge said to install all three, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 42 to 47 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk, the library and the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
151	<p>Five items are new rules: change 37, a program's trait with its own <code>+</code> excludes <code>Number</code>; change 39, a type without its own <code>=</code> compares by strict equivalence; change 40, the static argument written where walk's printed type misleads; change 41, <code>Number</code> declares nothing; change 42, walk's float numerals under strict equivalence. Change 38 narrows a rule: the <code>Index</code> bound goes only into code that walk runs or that the checker checks against the interpreter's library. The other five state facts that the batch changed, and change no rule.</p>
152	<p>Each item shows the text on <code>main</code> now, against the text before the first of the three commits. The words that came in are underlined, and the words that went are struck through.</p>
153	
154	<section id="l37" class="part item">
155	<h3><span class="num">Change 37 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Dispatch and overloading&rdquo;</span>A program's trait with its own + excludes Number</h3>
156	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
157	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Dispatch and overloading&rdquo;, line 24, the paragraph on the Meet Rule</p>
158	<p>The Meet Rule asks for a declaration that covers the meet of two overloads that neither exclude each other nor nest (<code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Fortress as a language&rdquo;). An abstract functional method also counts for it. So the checker refuses <code>h(self): ZZ32</code> in a trait <code>S</code> beside a top-level <code>h(x: String): ZZ32</code>: nothing covers the meet of <code>S</code> and <code>String</code>. <span class="add">Likewise, a program's trait that declares its own functional <code>+</code> overlaps the library's <code>+</code> between an array and a number, because a type can extend both the trait and <code>Number</code>, which has no <code>comprises</code> clause (P-160). Declare the trait <code>excludes { Number }</code>, or make it exclude arrays. Otherwise walk refuses the program at load: &ldquo;fails the Meet Rule&rdquo;. The checker refuses such a pair in one component, but accepts it against the library's <code>+</code> (row 918). Give a trait with its own functional <code>-</code>, <code>MIN</code> or <code>MAX</code> the same exclusion: the library declares these in the same form.</span></p>
159	</div></div>
160	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. Your decision opened the library's <code>Number</code>: the batch removed its <code>comprises</code> clause, so that a program may declare its own number type below it. Since then, one type can extend both <code>Number</code> and a program's own trait. So a trait that declares its own functional <code>+</code> overlaps the library's <code>+</code> between an array and a number, and walk refuses the whole program at load, with &ldquo;fails the Meet Rule&rdquo;. The checker accepts the same program (ledger row 918). The old text did not warn of this. The new sentences give the fix: the trait excludes <code>Number</code>, or arrays. They give <code>-</code>, <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> the same fix, since the library declares them in the same form. Only <code>+</code> was measured, but an extra exclusion cannot make a valid program invalid. The first wording gave the reason as &ldquo;<code>Number</code> is open&rdquo;, a term that the part did not define. After the cold read, it says that a type can extend both the trait and <code>Number</code>, which has no <code>comprises</code> clause.</p></div>
161	</section>
162	
163	<section id="l38" class="part item">
164	<h3><span class="num">Change 38 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>Where to write the Index bound</h3>
165	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
166	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, line 48, the paragraph on the <code>Index</code> bound: its first sentences, then its last sentence before its two departures; the sentences between did not change</p>
167	<p><strong>An indexed type's index parameter is bounded by <code>Index</code> (P-156).</strong> An indexed type is a type whose values <code>a[i]</code> subscripts, such as an array. Its index parameter is the static parameter that gives the type of <code>i</code>. In the library, and in your own generic over such a type, write <code>I extends Index</code>. Walk runs the code without the bound. The checker refuses it when it checks against the interpreter's library, as the count stage does (<code>compiler.md</code>). &hellip; Until the switch-over, the compiler's prelude declares no <code>Index</code>, so a program that names it does not compile<span class="cut">.</span><span class="add">: write the bound only in code that runs under walk or is checked against the interpreter's library.</span> Two departures:</p>
168	</div></div>
169	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A change of a rule's meaning: the rule now holds only where the tree can follow it. The paragraph told a worker to write <code>I extends Index</code> in its own generic over an indexed type. Its last sentence said that, until the switch-over, a program that names <code>Index</code> does not compile on the compiled path. So for a compiled program the two sentences gave opposite orders. The orientation update's own cold read found this. The last sentence now gives the action: write the bound only in code that runs under walk, or that the checker checks against the interpreter's library. The update wrote this as a sentence of its own, which began &ldquo;So write the bound now&rdquo;. After the skill's cold read, it became the end of the sentence before it, and the loose &ldquo;now&rdquo; went.</p></div>
170	</section>
