172	<section id="l39" class="part item">
173	<h3><span class="num">Change 39 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>A type without its own = compares by strict equivalence</h3>
174	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
175	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, lines 55 to 61, a new rule after the rule on pairs and triples</p>
176	<p><span class="add"><strong>Where a type declares no <code>=</code>, <code>=</code> is strict equivalence, <code>SEQV</code> (P-168).</strong> The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> answers <code>a SEQV b</code>. <code>SEQV</code> follows the specification on both paths:</span></p>
177	<ul>
178	<li><span class="add">values of different types are never equivalent;</span></li>
179	<li><span class="add">a value object is equivalent field by field, a tuple element by element, an integer, a character or a boolean by its value, and a float by its bits;</span></li>
180	<li><span class="add">an object not declared <code>value</code>, a string among them, is equivalent only to itself.</span></li>
181	</ul>
182	<p><span class="add">So for a value object <code>V</code> without its own <code>=</code>, <code>V(s) = V(t)</code> is <code>false</code> where <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> are equal strings made by two computations. The library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so <code>(s, 1, 2, 3) = (t, 1, 2, 3)</code> is <code>false</code> too. Both <code>false</code> answers are the rule's: assert them as correct. Under walk a float numeral is a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, so it is not equivalent to a computed <code>RR64</code> (<code>interpreter.md</code>, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;). To compare by content, give the value object an <code>=</code>, or compare the parts. A string's <code>=</code> compares text. Under walk, <code>=/=</code> and <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> reach a type's own <code>=</code> only if the type extends a library trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code> (row 807). Otherwise write <code>assert(x = y, msg)</code>. Until the switch-over, the compiler's library declares no <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> and none on tuples. Its own operator <code>===</code>, the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>, compares strings and <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code> by <code>=</code>, not by bits or identity (row 908).</span></p>
183	</div></div>
184	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. The batch made both paths answer <code>SEQV</code>, strict equivalence, by the specification's rule, as your decision on its meaning asks. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code>, which every type without its own <code>=</code> falls back to, answers <code>SEQV</code>. The old part said nothing of this. A worker would expect such an <code>=</code> to compare content, and would take the two <code>false</code> answers in the new text for defects. The new rule says how <code>SEQV</code> compares, gives those two cases and says to assert them as correct, and gives the fix. It also names walk's limit on <code>assert</code>, and what the compiler's library does until the switch-over. After the cold read, it says how an integer, a character or a boolean compares: by its value. It names a string's kind as &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo;, in place of &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;, which the skill does not define. It writes the two <code>false</code> cases as examples, in place of a string &ldquo;built apart&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply. It points to walk's float numerals (change 42). And it says what <code>===</code> is: the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>.</p></div>
185	</section>
