187	<section id="l40" class="part item">
188	<h3><span class="num">Change 40 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Walk prints ZZ32 where the instance is Int</h3>
189	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
190	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, line 88, the paragraph &ldquo;Under walk a numeral has a run-time class&rdquo;, up to its sentence on walk's error messages; the sentences after it did not change</p>
191	<p><strong>Under walk a numeral has a run-time class.</strong> The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling under <code>Number</code> that converts to the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> (P-76). Walk gives a numeral the class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> by its value. These classes stand for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, but a generic instantiates at the class<span class="cut">: <code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code></span><span class="add">. Walk prints these classes as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside static arguments, so a printed type does not show the instance: <code>Cell(3)</code> prints <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> but is a <code>Cell[\Int\]</code>, and no <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code></span> (row 364). <span class="add">Where you need a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, write <code>Cell[\ZZ32\](3)</code>. Walk's own error messages say <code>Int</code>.</span></p>
192	</div></div>
193	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule, beside a fact that changed. The batch changed how walk prints a type: it now prints its numeral classes <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> and <code>BigNum</code> as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside a static argument. The old example, &ldquo;<code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code>&rdquo;, named a type that walk no longer prints. A worker who saw <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> in walk's output would take the value for a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, and a dispatch on that type would then fail. The text now says that the printed name hides the instance. After the cold read, it says what to write where a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> is needed, the workaround that ledger row 364 records. It names the printed names outright, in place of &ldquo;the traits' names&rdquo;. And the sentence on walk's error messages lost a &ldquo;still&rdquo;, which told of a change rather than of what a worker sees.</p></div>
194	</section>
