196	<section id="l41" class="part item">
197	<h3><span class="num">Change 41 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Number declares nothing</h3>
198	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
199	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, line 92, a new rule after the rule on coercions</p>
200	<p><span class="add"><strong><code>Number</code> declares nothing (P-165).</strong> Each of the eight number types, and <code>IntLiteral</code>, declares its own <code>asFloat</code> into <code>RR64</code>. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call on a <code>T</code> no method or operator that the number types declare, <code>asFloat</code> among them. Two numbers of different types compare by <code>=</code> only where a coercion carries one into the other: the library declares <code>=</code> for each such pair, so a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value are <code>=</code>. Otherwise <code>=</code> is <code>SEQV</code> (&ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;), and answers <code>false</code> without an error: <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>false</code>, as is a <code>ZZ64</code> against an equal <code>RR64</code>. To compare values across types at known types, write <code>asFloat(x) = y</code>. In generic code over <code>T extends Number</code>, declare your function once for each number type instead. A program may also declare its own number type below <code>Number</code> (P-160).</span></p>
201	</div></div>
202	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. By your decision, <code>asFloat</code>, and the <code>=</code> built on it, left the trait <code>Number</code>, and the batch made it so. Each of the eight number types, and <code>IntLiteral</code>, now declares its own <code>asFloat</code>, and <code>Number</code> declares nothing. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call nothing on a <code>T</code> that the number types declare. Two numbers of different types are <code>=</code> only where a coercion joins them. Otherwise <code>=</code> falls back to <code>SEQV</code> and answers <code>false</code>, with no error. The old part said none of this: a worker would have called <code>asFloat</code> on a <code>T</code>, or expected <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> to be <code>true</code>. After the cold read, the text gives a pair that a coercion joins, a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value, which are <code>=</code>. It says &ldquo;declare your function once for each number type&rdquo;, since &ldquo;the function&rdquo; could be read as <code>asFloat</code>. And its last sentence no longer says why a program may declare its own number type: change 37 now gives that reason.</p></div>
203	</section>
