232	<section id="l45" class="part item">
233	<h3><span class="num">Change 45 &middot; compiler.md, &ldquo;What the compiled path cannot compile yet&rdquo;</span>The code generator compiles a type assumption, but for three forms</h3>
234	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
235	<p class="where-in">compiler.md, &ldquo;What the compiled path cannot compile yet&rdquo;, lines 50 to 54, a new paragraph and list after the forms of local function that the code generator refuses</p>
236	<p><span class="add">A type assumption <code>e asif T</code> has the value of <code>e</code>, but the call around it chooses its declaration as if <code>e</code> were a <code>T</code>, a supertype of <code>e</code>'s type (<code>Specification/basic/expressions/type-annotation.tex</code>). The code generator compiles one at a dotted call's receiver, at a call of a functional method or an overloaded function whose every argument is an assumption, and elsewhere as the value of <code>e</code>. It refuses these forms (ledger row 818):</span></p>
237	<ul>
238	<li><span class="add">a call of a functional method or an overloaded function with an assumption at some of its arguments but not all: &lsquo;Can't compile a dispatched call whose arguments are not all type assumptions&rsquo;;</span></li>
239	<li><span class="add">a generic method on an assumed receiver: &lsquo;Can't compile a type assumption at the receiver of a generic method yet&rsquo;;</span></li>
240	<li><span class="add">a call of an overloaded generic function with an assumption at an argument: &lsquo;Can't compile a type assumption at a call of an overloaded generic function yet&rsquo;.</span></li>
241	</ul>
242	</div></div>
243	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the code generator learned a new form. A type assumption, <code>e asif T</code>, keeps the value of <code>e</code>, but makes the call around it choose its declaration as if <code>e</code> were of the supertype <code>T</code>. The batch taught the code generator to compile one at a dotted call's receiver, at a call whose arguments are all assumptions, and anywhere else as the plain value. It refuses three forms, each with its own message. The part said nothing of type assumptions. A worker would have kept them out of compiled code, or recorded a refusal as some other failure. After the cold read, the part defines a type assumption from the specification. It names the calls that need an assumption at every argument or at none: a call of a functional method or of an overloaded function. The first wording said &ldquo;every dispatched argument&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply, since a Fortress call dispatches on every argument. And each refused form is named by the call that holds it.</p></div>
244	</section>
