246	<section id="l46" class="part item">
247	<h3><span class="num">Change 46 &middot; compiler.md, &ldquo;What the code generator does&rdquo;</span>The value modifier marks the compiled class</h3>
248	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
249	<p class="where-in">compiler.md, &ldquo;What the code generator does&rdquo;, line 68, its third bullet</p>
250	<ul><li>The <code>value</code> modifier <span class="cut">has no effect on the representation.</span> <span class="add">adds the mark interface <code>ValueObjectMark</code> to the object's class. The compiled <code>SEQV</code> (<code>nativeHelpers/equality.java</code>) reads the mark. The modifier does not inline or unbox the fields.</span></li></ul>
251	</div></div>
252	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the fact changed. The bullet said that the <code>value</code> modifier has no effect on how the code generator represents an object. The batch made the code generator add a mark interface, <code>ValueObjectMark</code>, to a value object's class, and the compiled <code>SEQV</code> reads it to compare such objects field by field. A worker who believed the old bullet could remove the mark, or miss why <code>SEQV</code> fails without it. After the cold read, the bullet names the file that holds the compiled <code>SEQV</code>. It says that the modifier does not inline or unbox the fields, since &ldquo;the fields stay as they are&rdquo; read two ways.</p></div>
253	</section>
