259	<p>The count stage takes <code>&lt;out-file&gt; &lt;scratch&gt;</code> and about <span class="cut">150</span> <span class="add">80 to 180</span> seconds, more under load. <span class="cut">That is longer than</span> <span class="add">It can run past</span> the Bash tool's <span class="cut">default time</span> <span class="add">120-second</span> limit, so run it in the background (<code>session.md</code>).</p>
260	</div></div>
261	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the time changed, and the order to run the stage in the background stays. From climb batch 22 to climb batch 26, the count stage took about 150 to 180 seconds. In climb batch 27's checks before its landing, it took about 80 seconds, and no run measured why. The text now gives the range. The first wording kept &ldquo;That is longer than the Bash tool's default time limit&rdquo;, which is false at 80 seconds. A worker who read it so could run the stage in the foreground, and the tool would stop a 180-second run at 120 seconds. After the cold read, the sentence says that the stage can run past the tool's 120-second limit, so it runs in the background.</p></div>
