276	<section id="words" class="part">
277	<h2>Words used on this page</h2>
278	<dl class="gloss">
279	<dt>coordinator</dt><dd>Coordinating session.</dd>
280	<dt>diff</dt><dd>Text that shows what a change cut and added.</dd>
281	<dt><code>fortress-repo</code></dt><dd>Skill that holds the rules that bind the workers in the repository.</dd>
282	<dt>skill writer</dt><dd>Headless session that makes every edit of the project skills.</dd>
283	<dt>cold read</dt><dd>Reading of a change by a worker new to the repository, which flags each passage that would send a worker wrong or make it search.</dd>
284	<dt>orientation update</dt><dd>Workflow's rewrite of <code>revival-changes.md</code> after a landing, which a judge checks section by section before the skill writer installs it.</dd>
285	<dt><code>revival-changes.md</code></dt><dd>Part of the <code>fortress-repo</code> skill that states what the revival changed in the team's Fortress, which every task on the tree's code reads whole.</dd>
286	<dt>record-landing workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that lands a batch&rsquo;s results in the record and the skills, and updates this page.</dd>
287	<dt>provenance line</dt><dd>Line of <code>references/sources.md</code> that records where a sentence of the skill came from.</dd>
288	<dt>walk</dt><dd>Fortress interpreter, which runs a program from its syntax tree.</dd>
289	<dt>checker</dt><dd>Static type checker of the compiled path.</dd>
290	<dt>runtime</dt><dd>System that runs a compiled program and holds its values: the class loader, the value classes and the task and transaction code under <code>runtimeSystem/</code>.</dd>
291	<dt>compiled path</dt><dd>Way of running a program through the compiled checker, the code generator and the runtime, as against walk.</dd>
292	<dt>code generator</dt><dd>Part of the compiler that turns a checked program into bytecode.</dd>
293	<dt>switch-over</dt><dd>Planned move of the compiled path onto the interpreter's library.</dd>
294	<dt>interpreter's library</dt><dd>Library under <code>Library/</code> that walk reads, onto which the switch-over moves the compiled path.</dd>
295	<dt>compiler's library, compiler's prelude</dt><dd>Library that the compiled path reads until the switch-over.</dd>
296	<dt>ledger row</dt><dd>Numbered entry of the gap ledger, the list of the known gaps of the tree.</dd>
297	<dt>count stage</dt><dd>Stage of the measure of the distance to the switch-over that checks the interpreter's library with the compiled checker and counts its errors.</dd>
298	<dt>functional method</dt><dd>Method that a call names as a function, with <code>self</code> among its arguments, as an operator of a trait.</dd>
299	<dt>native</dt><dd>Function that the library binds to Java code by a body <code>builtinPrimitive("…")</code>.</dd>
300	<dt>binding</dt><dd>Library declaration whose body names a native's Java code.</dd>
301	<dt>helper</dt><dd>Public static Java method under <code>nativeHelpers/</code> that runs a native on both paths.</dd>
302	<dt>Meet Rule</dt><dd>Overloading rule that asks, for two overloads that neither exclude each other nor nest, a declaration over the meet of their parameter types, the types that both admit.</dd>
303	<dt><code>comprises</code> clause</dt><dd>Clause of a trait that lists every type that may directly extend it, and so closes the trait to other types.</dd>
304	<dt><code>excludes</code> clause</dt><dd>Clause of a trait that names types that no value of the trait may also have.</dd>
305	<dt><code>Number</code></dt><dd>Library's trait above the number types.</dd>
306	<dt>number types</dt><dd>Eight types of the library below <code>Number</code>: <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, integers of 32 bits, 64 bits and any size; <code>NN32</code> and <code>NN64</code>, unsigned integers; <code>QQ</code>, rationals; <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code>, floats of 32 and 64 bits.</dd>
307	<dt><code>IntLiteral</code></dt><dd>Checker's type of an integer numeral, below <code>Number</code>, which converts to the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code>.</dd>
308	<dt><code>FloatLiteral</code></dt><dd>Walk's type of a float numeral, such as <code>0.5</code>, below <code>RR64</code> but not <code>RR64</code> itself.</dd>
309	<dt>coercion</dt><dd>Conversion of a value from one type into another that the library declares, and that a call or a binding applies by itself.</dd>
310	<dt>static argument</dt><dd>Type in <code>[\ \]</code> that instantiates a generic, as <code>ZZ32</code> in <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>.</dd>
311	<dt><code>SEQV</code></dt><dd>Operator of strict equivalence, which tells whether two values are the same value.</dd>
312	<dt>value object</dt><dd>Object declared with the modifier <code>value</code>, which <code>SEQV</code> compares field by field.</dd>
313	<dt><code>Equality</code></dt><dd>Library trait that declares <code>=</code>.</dd>
314	<dt>type assumption</dt><dd>Expression <code>e asif T</code>, which has the value of <code>e</code> while the call around it chooses its declaration as if <code>e</code> were a <code>T</code>.</dd>
315	<dt>mark interface</dt><dd>Java interface with no methods, which a class implements so that code can recognize the class.</dd>
316	</dl>
